From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 19:07:38 -0643
Subject: [PATCH] spi: s3c64xx: turn the channels off before clearing the
 packet count

s3c64xx_flush_fifo() clears PACKET_CNT, and with it PACKET_CNT_EN, while
RXCH_ON is still set. For the duration of that write the controller is
receiving with no packet count, so it runs SCK freely; with automatic
chip select the slave stays selected and shifts out bits that the flush
then throws away.

Slaves that restart their protocol on every chip-select cycle don't
notice. A slave that streams across cycles does: the Pixel 9a's GNSS
core, which talks CHPP over this controller, lost two to six bits per
transfer, so every read arrived at a different bit offset and frames that
crossed reads failed their CRC. With the channels turned off first, as
Google's driver for this controller does, every read arrives at bit 0
with a valid CRC and the receiver gets a fix.

Turn RX and TX off before clearing the packet count.

Link: https://android.googlesource.com/kernel/google-modules/soc/gs/+/204078758737776959ecc85c527a00fd2cabb62f/drivers/spi/spi-s3c64xx.c
Signed-off-by: W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/spi/spi-s3c64xx.c | 9 +++++++--
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/spi/spi-s3c64xx.c b/drivers/spi/spi-s3c64xx.c
index 870aae659..185de17f6 100644
--- a/drivers/spi/spi-s3c64xx.c
+++ b/drivers/spi/spi-s3c64xx.c
@@ -235,12 +235,17 @@ static void s3c64xx_flush_fifo(struct s3c64xx_spi_driver_data *sdd)
 	unsigned long loops;
 	u32 val;
 
-	writel(0, regs + S3C64XX_SPI_PACKET_CNT);
-
 	val = readl(regs + S3C64XX_SPI_CH_CFG);
 	val &= ~(S3C64XX_SPI_CH_RXCH_ON | S3C64XX_SPI_CH_TXCH_ON);
 	writel(val, regs + S3C64XX_SPI_CH_CFG);
 
+	/*
+	 * Clear the count only with the channels off, as Google's driver does:
+	 * with RXCH_ON set and PACKET_CNT_EN cleared the controller clocks
+	 * freely, and with automatic chip select the slave sees those edges.
+	 */
+	writel(0, regs + S3C64XX_SPI_PACKET_CNT);
+
 	val = readl(regs + S3C64XX_SPI_CH_CFG);
 	val |= S3C64XX_SPI_CH_SW_RST;
 	val &= ~S3C64XX_SPI_CH_HS_EN;
